"John H." <JohnWH@gmail.com> writes:
> But the problem is less finding an arbitrary source of points than
> surviving long enough to do so, and having the patience to do it
> often enough to get those absurd scores.  This is the entire reason
> score [overhauls] are being proposed.

If there is a simple solution to the alleged score problem, its quest
is blazoned in such things as awarding points for variety of player
behavior rather than repetition of a few actions.

Killing your first endgame rider is much more significant than killing
your first troll. Killing death a fifth time is much less exciting
than your first newt.

I'd also not mind seeing score awards for "collect the whole set".
Example: Pick some score award for identifying all gemstones, say 100K
points. Cut it in half for each one you haven't ID'ed (or have
forgotten) when the game ends. ID all but three? Get 12500 points.
(Classes who start with a type of item auto-ID'ed would be ineligable
for the award for that type.)

A similar scheme (with, IMHO, a large award) could apply to collecting
useful intrinsics, defeating different uniques, and defeating types of
non-uniques.

In a larger sense, though, perhaps the same amount of development
effort would be better spent adding new _content_ to the game. A few
more special levels as randomly-selected alternatives to Sokoban, and
perhaps adding a bit of spice to Gehennom would be obvious
choices. ("If you want Slash'em you know where to find it.": hush.)

